select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b02-016","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"The term 'standards' in an energy-efficiency programme has two meanings. What are they?","back":"a) Well-defined test protocols that give an accurate estimate, or relative ranking, of a product's energy performance. b) Target limits on energy performance (maximum use or minimum efficiency) based on a specified test protocol.","explain":"Standards can also prohibit the sale of products below a minimum efficiency level. Labels, by contrast, are information affixed to products so consumers can make informed choices.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p56","order":1083},
{"id":"p1-b02-017","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish comparative labels from endorsement labels.","back":"Comparative labels let consumers compare the efficiency of all models of a product (relative energy use, e.g. star ratings). Endorsement labels mark a group of products as efficient when they meet the minimum criteria in the product schedule.","explain":"The BEE star label is a comparative label. An endorsement label is a 'seal of approval' type that does not rank models against each other.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p56","order":1084},
{"id":"p1-b02-018","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What do Minimum Energy Performance Standards (MEPS) specify, and what do they not specify?","back":"They specify minimum efficiency (or maximum energy consumption) that every product must achieve, that is, energy performance or output. They do not specify the technology or design details.","explain":"Keeping MEPS technology-neutral lets manufacturers innovate in how they meet the level. MEPS are reviewed and raised periodically so that more efficient products become available.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p56","order":1085},
{"id":"p1-b02-019","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"In BEE's star rating, which star level is the most energy efficient?","back":"5 stars is the most efficient; 1 star is the least efficient.","explain":"The number of stars depends on the highest pre-set energy performance threshold the appliance meets. More stars means more savings. Star rating is part of BEE's S&L programme.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p56","order":1086},
{"id":"p1-b02-020","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Which four appliances came under mandatory labelling from 7 January 2010?","back":"Frost-free (household) refrigerators, room air conditioners, tubular fluorescent lamps, and distribution transformers (up to 200 kVA).","explain":"In the 11th Plan the S&L programme covered 18 equipment types. Others such as direct-cool refrigerators, induction motors, ceiling fans, agricultural pump sets, colour TVs, geysers, LPG stoves, DG sets and washing machines were voluntary at the time of the 2015 book.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p57","order":1087},
{"id":"p1-b02-021","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"problem","front":"A 5-star 220 L refrigerator costs Rs 17,000 and uses 204 kWh/yr. A non-star model costs Rs 10,000 and uses 520 kWh/yr. With electricity at Rs 5/kWh, find the annual energy and money savings and the simple payback on the extra cost.","back":"Saves 316 kWh/yr and Rs 1,580/yr; simple payback on the Rs 7,000 extra cost ≈ 4.4 years.","explain":"This is the guide book's star-rating example. Its table prints a payback of 1–1.5 years, but the book's own figures (Rs 7,000 extra ÷ Rs 1,580/yr) give about 4.4 years. The book notes the prices and consumption are assumed values for illustration.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p58","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the annual energy saving.","answer":"520 − 204 = 316 kWh/yr","explain":"Difference in rated annual consumption."},{"prompt":"Find the annual cost of each model.","answer":"Star: 204 × 5 = Rs 1,020; non-star: 520 × 5 = Rs 2,600","explain":"Energy × tariff."},{"prompt":"Find the annual money saving.","answer":"2,600 − 1,020 = Rs 1,580/yr","explain":"Equivalently 316 × 5."},{"prompt":"Find the extra first cost.","answer":"17,000 − 10,000 = Rs 7,000","explain":"Investment for the efficient choice."},{"prompt":"Compute simple payback.","answer":"7,000 ÷ 1,580 ≈ 4.4 years","explain":"Payback = extra investment ÷ annual saving. The book's table shows 1–1.5 years, which does not follow from these figures."}],"order":1088},
{"id":"p1-b02-022","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What saving did BEE's pilot study show for replacing inefficient agricultural pumps with star-rated pump sets, and how is Ag-DSM implemented?","back":"About 40% energy saving. Pump-set replacement is carried out by an ESCO or the distribution company on rural feeders where supply-quality improvements (feeder segregation, HVDS) have already been done.","explain":"DSM means utilities managing customer demand, for example shifting load from peak to off-peak, to cut expensive peak purchases and capital needs. BEE's municipal DSM programme covers 175 municipalities with investment-grade audits and DPRs, with ESCOs encouraged to implement.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p58","order":1089},
{"id":"p1-b02-023","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Explain the Bachat Lamp Yojana (BLY): what it replaced, at what price, and how the price gap was funded.","back":"It replaced household incandescent bulbs with CFLs sold at about Rs 15 each (similar to a bulb's price), using the Clean Development Mechanism (CDM) to recover the gap between the CFL's market price and Rs 15.","explain":"60 W and 100 W bulbs were replaced by 11–15 W and 20–25 W CFLs, giving about 80% energy saving with no loss of light. The major part of the subsidy comes from CDM revenue (carbon credits), not from DISCOMs or the central budget.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p59","order":1090},
{"id":"p1-b02-024","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What is the objective of BEE's SME programme, and name example clusters.","back":"To speed up adoption of energy-efficient technologies and practices in selected SME clusters through knowledge sharing, capacity building and innovative financing. It is market driven. Examples: Ahmedabad (chemicals), Surat (textiles), Warangal (rice milling).","explain":"It funds need-based diagnostic studies and pilot projects at cluster level, builds the capacity of local service and technology providers, and facilitates implementation through collateral funding by lead banks.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p59","order":1091},
{"id":"p1-b02-025","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"State the annual energy consumption thresholds for designated consumers in these sectors: thermal power, fertilizer, cement, iron & steel, pulp & paper, chlor-alkali, aluminium, textile.","back":"30,000 toe/yr for thermal power, fertilizer, cement, iron & steel, and pulp & paper; 12,000 toe for chlor-alkali; 7,500 toe for aluminium; 3,000 toe for textile.","explain":"Nine energy-intensive sectors were notified, the ninth being Railways (traction sub-stations, loco sheds, production units and workshops). DCs must appoint a certified energy manager, get mandatory audits, comply with norms and report energy use every financial year.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p60","order":1092},
{"id":"p1-b02-026","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"For working out a designated consumer's energy use in MTOE, what calorific basis is used, and what if the supplier gives no GCV certificate?","back":"1 kg oil equivalent = 10,000 kcal and 1 MTOE = 1 × 10⁷ kcal. Without a supplier certificate, the GCV is taken from a test certificate of a NABL-accredited, State Government or Government-recognised laboratory.","explain":"Using 10,000 kcal/kg as the oil-equivalent basis is a common MCQ (not 10,500 or 9,800). Actual fuel GCVs are multiplied by quantities and divided by 10⁷ to get MTOE.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p60","order":1093},
{"id":"p1-b02-027","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What is the Perform, Achieve and Trade (PAT) scheme, and how many DCs did its first cycle cover?","back":"A market-based mechanism that sets mandatory specific energy consumption (SEC) reduction targets for energy-intensive DCs and lets units that beat their targets earn tradable energy savings certificates (ESCerts). The first cycle covered 478 DCs in 8 sectors.","explain":"Sector counts: thermal power 144, textile 90, cement 85, iron & steel 67, pulp & paper 31, fertilizer 29, chlor-alkali 22, aluminium 10. The legal basis is the 2010 amendment (Section 14A).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p60","order":1094},
{"id":"p1-b02-028","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"How were PAT cycle-1 targets set, when was the assessment year, and how are ESCerts traded?","back":"Targets were unit-specific, about 3–7% SEC reduction: smaller for already-efficient units and larger for less-efficient ones. The assessment year was 2014-15. ESCerts trade on power exchanges and can be banked for the next cycle.","explain":"PAT cycle 1 started in 2012-13 and lasted three years. ESCerts issued depend on savings beyond the target in the assessment year. Units missing targets face penalty under Section 26(1A), and compliance is checked by verification and check-verification through empanelled accredited energy auditor firms.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p61","order":1095},
{"id":"p1-b02-029","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What qualifications does a person need to become an Accredited Energy Auditor?","back":"Be a certified energy manager who has also passed BEE's 'Energy Performance for Equipment and Utility Systems' exam; have five years of energy audit experience, at least three in energy-intensive industries; and receive a certificate of accreditation from BEE.","explain":"BEE's Accreditation Advisory Committee assesses the applicant's audit experience and competence through an oral interview before granting accreditation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p62","order":1096},
{"id":"p1-b02-030","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Which earlier laws did the Electricity Act 2003 consolidate?","back":"The Indian Electricity Act 1910, the Electricity (Supply) Act 1948 and the Electricity Regulatory Commissions Act 1998.","explain":"The Act aims to develop the electricity sector by distancing government from regulation. It consolidates the laws on generation, transmission, distribution, trading and use of electricity. The EC Act 2001, not the Electricity Act, gives the legal framework for energy efficiency.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p63","order":1097},
{"id":"p1-b02-031","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"List five main features of the Electricity Act 2003.","back":"Any five of: generation free from licensing; captive generation free from control; restructuring of SEBs; mandatory regulatory commissions; open access in transmission; phased open access in distribution; trading recognised as a distinct activity; strict provisions on grid discipline and power theft; supply to all areas with rural provisions; rationalised tariffs.","explain":"Exceptions and controls remain. CEA clearance is still needed for hydro projects (dam safety, inter-state issues), load dispatch stays with government for grid stability, and metering of all supply is mandatory.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p63","order":1098},
{"id":"p1-b02-032","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What is the Renewable Purchase Obligation (RPO), who sets it, and how can it be met?","back":"A quota set by State Electricity Regulatory Commissions under the Electricity Act 2003. Each retail seller must buy a minimum share of power from renewable sources such as wind, solar, small hydro and biomass. It can be met by owning a renewable facility or by purchasing renewable power.","explain":"Internationally it is called a Renewable Portfolio Standard (RPS): a policy that guarantees a minimum amount of renewable energy in the resource portfolio.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p64","order":1099},
{"id":"p1-b02-033","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"According to the Integrated Energy Policy (Expert Committee, August 2006), what power capacity would India need by 2031-32?","back":"Nearly 8,00,000 MW, up from about 1,60,000 MW at the time, with coal demand over 2 billion t/yr.","explain":"To sustain 8% growth to 2031-32 and meet lifeline needs, primary energy supply must grow 3–4 times and electricity capacity or supply 6–7 times over 2003-04 levels. Coal remains the most important source through 2031-32, so clean-coal and in-situ gasification technologies are stressed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p66","order":1100},
{"id":"p1-b02-034","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What energy intensities does the Integrated Energy Policy quote for India and other economies, and by how much could India cut its intensity?","back":"India 0.16 kgoe per dollar of GDP, against China 0.23, the US 0.22, the world 0.21, and Germany/OECD 0.17. Denmark (0.13), the UK (0.14), and Brazil and Japan (0.15) do better. Efficiency could cut India's energy intensity by up to 25%.","explain":"The policy treats lower energy intensity through efficiency as an untapped domestic energy source. Key areas include mining, generation, T&D, water pumping, industrial processes, haulage, mass transport and building design.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p67","order":1101},
{"id":"p1-b02-035","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What power-plant efficiency targets and procurement principle does the Integrated Energy Policy set?","back":"Raise average gross generation efficiency from 30.5% to 34%. New plants should move from 36% to at least 38–40%. Government and PSUs should buy on minimum life-cycle cost rather than minimum initial cost.","explain":"Other measures: benchmarks for all energy-intensive sectors, minimum fuel-efficiency standards for vehicles, urban mass transport and rail freight, and energy-efficiency specialisations and certification in technical colleges.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p67","order":1102},
{"id":"p1-b02-036","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Under the Integrated Energy Policy, what should renewable-energy incentives be linked to, and what instrument was proposed?","back":"Actual energy generated, not capacity installed. The proposed instrument was a Tradable Tax Rebate Certificate (TTRC), payable according to certified energy actually supplied.","explain":"Other measures include feed-in laws or differential tariffs for wind, small hydro and cogeneration; RPS; and promoting fuel-wood plantations, biogas, wood gasifiers, solar thermal and PV, biodiesel and ethanol. The main long-term focus is solar.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p70","order":1103},
{"id":"p1-b02-037","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"How many days of oil imports does the Integrated Energy Policy recommend holding as a strategic reserve?","back":"A reserve equal to 90 days of oil imports (and/or buy options on emergency supplies from large storages such as Singapore).","explain":"Other energy-security recommendations: more exploration for coal, oil and gas; buffer stocks for short-term price volatility; in-situ coal gasification; coal-bed methane; enhanced oil recovery; the thorium cycle; and solar power. Redundancy guards against technical risks such as generator failure or pipeline leaks.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p71","order":1104},
{"id":"p1-b02-038","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three stages of India's planned nuclear power development?","back":"Pressurised heavy water reactors → fast breeder reactors → thorium-based reactors.","explain":"India has poor uranium resources (ore up to 0.1% U against 12–14% abroad), enough for only about 10,000 MW, which makes nuclear power 2–3 times costlier than with imported supplies. Thorium is the long-term route.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p72","order":1105},
{"id":"p1-b02-039","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Name the eight National Missions under the National Action Plan on Climate Change (2008).","back":"National Solar Mission; Enhanced Energy Efficiency; Sustainable Habitat; Water; Sustaining the Himalayan Ecosystem; Green India; Sustainable Agriculture; Strategic Knowledge for Climate Change.","explain":"A favourite MCQ trap: there is no 'National Mission on CFC alternatives'. NAPCC was released in 2008 to advance development while addressing adaptation and mitigation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p73","order":1106},
{"id":"p1-b02-040","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What are the four initiatives of the National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE)?","back":"PAT (Perform, Achieve and Trade); MTEE (Market Transformation for Energy Efficiency); EEFP (Energy Efficiency Financing Platform); FEEED (Framework for Energy Efficient Economic Development).","explain":"PAT certifies and trades industrial savings. MTEE makes efficient appliances affordable (BLY, SEEP). EEFP links financial institutions, ESCOs and government to finance DSM. FEEED offers fiscal instruments: the Partial Risk Guarantee Fund (PRGFEE) and the Venture Capital Fund (VCFEE) for energy efficiency.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p74","order":1107},
{"id":"p1-b02-041","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What were NMEEE's expected outcomes at full implementation?","back":"Avoided capacity of 19,598 MW, fuel savings of about 23 million t/yr, and GHG reductions of 98.55 million t/yr, unlocking an energy-efficiency market of about Rs 74,000 crore.","explain":"NMEEE was approved for 2010-11 and 2011-12 with an outlay of Rs 235.50 crore and continued in the XII Plan with Rs 775 crore. Earlier EC Act schemes had already avoided 10,836 MW of capacity in the XI Plan.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p74","order":1108},
{"id":"p1-b02-042","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What targets do the Green India Mission and the National Water Mission set?","back":"Green India: afforest 6 million hectares and raise forest and tree cover from 23% to 33% of land area. Water Mission: improve water-use efficiency by 20%.","explain":"Other NAPCC facts: the Solar Mission notes 1% of India's land could meet its electricity needs till 2031. The Sustainable Habitat Mission covers building efficiency (extending ECBC), urban waste management and a shift to public transport.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p76","order":1109},
{"id":"p1-b02-043","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"problem","front":"A textile plant uses per month 7,00,000 kWh of grid electricity, 40 kL of furnace oil (SG 0.92, GCV 10,000 kcal/kg), 60 t of coal (GCV 3,450 kcal/kg) and 10 kL of HSD (SG 0.885, GCV 10,500 kcal/kg). Take 1 kWh = 860 kcal and 1 kgoe = 10,000 kcal. Find (a) monthly MTOE, (b) % share of each source, (c) whether the plant is a designated consumer.","back":"(a) ≈127 MTOE/month; (b) electricity 47.4%, furnace oil 29.0%, coal 16.3%, HSD 7.3%; (c) 1,524 MTOE/yr is below the 3,000 MTOE textile threshold, so it is not a designated consumer.","explain":"Convert each energy stream to kcal (volume × SG × GCV for liquids, kWh × 860 for electricity), add them and divide by 10⁷. Compare annual consumption with the notified threshold for the sector; for textiles this is 3,000 toe/yr.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p78","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert furnace oil to kcal.","answer":"40,000 L × 0.92 = 36,800 kg × 10,000 = 36.8 × 10⁷ kcal","explain":"Mass = volume × specific gravity (kg/L)."},{"prompt":"Convert coal to kcal.","answer":"60,000 kg × 3,450 = 20.7 × 10⁷ kcal","explain":"Energy = mass × GCV."},{"prompt":"Convert electricity to kcal.","answer":"7,00,000 × 860 = 60.2 × 10⁷ kcal","explain":"1 kWh = 860 kcal for the MTOE calculation."},{"prompt":"Convert HSD to kcal.","answer":"10,000 L × 0.885 = 8,850 kg × 10,500 = 9.29 × 10⁷ kcal","explain":"Same method as furnace oil."},{"prompt":"Add and convert to MTOE; find shares.","answer":"(36.8 + 20.7 + 60.2 + 9.29) × 10⁷ / 10⁷ ≈ 127 MTOE/month; shares 60.2/127 = 47.4%, 36.8/127 = 29.0%, 20.7/127 = 16.3%, 9.29/127 = 7.3%","explain":"1 MTOE = 10⁷ kcal."},{"prompt":"Check designated-consumer status.","answer":"127 × 12 ≈ 1,524 MTOE/yr < 3,000 MTOE → not a DC","explain":"The textile threshold is 3,000 toe/yr."}],"order":1110},
{"id":"p1-b02-044","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"problem","front":"An aluminium unit uses 60 million kWh of grid electricity and 1,500 t of furnace oil (GCV 10,000 kcal/kg) per year. Using 1 kWh = 860 kcal and 1 MTOE = 10⁷ kcal, is it a designated consumer?","back":"Total ≈ 6,660 MTOE/yr, below the 7,500 MTOE aluminium threshold, so it is not a designated consumer at this level.","explain":"This uses the same MTOE method as the guide book's solved example. The answer depends on the sector: 6,660 toe would make a textile (3,000) unit a DC, but not an aluminium (7,500) or cement (30,000) unit.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p60","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert electricity to MTOE.","answer":"60 × 10⁶ kWh × 860 = 5.16 × 10¹⁰ kcal ÷ 10⁷ = 5,160 MTOE","explain":"1 kWh = 860 kcal."},{"prompt":"Convert furnace oil to MTOE.","answer":"1,500,000 kg × 10,000 = 1.5 × 10¹⁰ kcal ÷ 10⁷ = 1,500 MTOE","explain":"Energy = mass × GCV."},{"prompt":"Find the total.","answer":"5,160 + 1,500 = 6,660 MTOE/yr","explain":"Add all energy streams."},{"prompt":"Compare with the threshold.","answer":"Aluminium threshold = 7,500 MTOE/yr → 6,660 < 7,500 → not a DC","explain":"Thresholds are sector-specific."}],"order":1111},
{"id":"p1-b02-045","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"problem","front":"A designated consumer fails to comply with a direction under Section 14(h) (getting an energy audit done), and the failure continues for 20 days after it is first penalised. What is the maximum total penalty under Section 26(1) as given in the guide book?","back":"₹10 lakh + 20 × ₹10,000 = ₹12 lakh (maximum).","explain":"Section 26(1) sets up to ₹10 lakh for each failure plus up to ₹10,000 per day of continuing failure. These are maximums decided by the adjudicating officer, and unpaid amounts are recovered as arrears of land revenue. Figures are from the 2010 text in the 2015 book.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p53","steps":[{"prompt":"Identify the applicable provision.","answer":"Section 26(1): failure under Section 14(h) is listed","explain":"26(1A) applies only to failure under 14(n), the energy norms."},{"prompt":"Apply the base penalty.","answer":"Up to ₹10,00,000","explain":"Maximum per failure."},{"prompt":"Apply the continuing-failure penalty.","answer":"20 days × ₹10,000 = ₹2,00,000","explain":"Up to ₹10,000 per day."},{"prompt":"Add them.","answer":"₹12,00,000 maximum","explain":"Recoverable as arrear of land revenue if unpaid."}],"order":1112},
{"id":"p1-013","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"1 tonne of oil equivalent (toe) equals how many kcal and MJ?","back":"1 toe = 10⁷ kcal = 41,868 MJ (41.868 GJ).","explain":"toe is the common unit BEE uses to add up different fuels; for example Designated Consumer thresholds and PAT targets are in toe. Convert each fuel to kcal (quantity × GCV) and divide by 10⁷. For example, 2,500 kg of coal at 4,000 kcal/kg = 10⁷ kcal = 1 toe.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q6; BEE 2024 P1 Q33","order":1113},
{"id":"p1-014","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What are the energy equivalents of 1 kWh and 1 kcal?","back":"1 kWh = 860 kcal = 3,600 kJ (3.6 MJ); 1 kcal = 4.187 kJ.","explain":"These conversions are used in almost every Paper 1 numerical. To convert a thermal load in kcal/h to electrical kW, divide by 860. To convert electrical energy to toe, multiply kWh by 860 and divide by 10⁷.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S5, S8","order":1114},
{"id":"p1-015","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Which of these is NOT equal to 1 atm: 101.3 kPa, 10,332 mmWC, 14.7 psi, 0.98 kg/cm²?","back":"0.98 kg/cm² is wrong. 1 atm ≈ 1.033 kg/cm² (101.325 kPa = 10,332 mmWC = 14.7 psi = 760 mmHg).","explain":"1 kg/cm² ≈ 0.98 bar, which is easy to confuse. Remember 1 atm = 1.01325 bar ≈ 1.033 kg/cm², slightly more than 1 kg/cm².","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q2","order":1115},
{"id":"p1-016","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"State the relation between gauge pressure, absolute (system) pressure and atmospheric pressure.","back":"Gauge pressure = Absolute (system) pressure − Atmospheric pressure, i.e. pg = ps − pa.","explain":"Pressure gauges read relative to the surrounding atmosphere, so a gauge open to air reads zero. Steam tables use absolute pressure, so add about 1.013 bar to a gauge reading before looking up properties.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q31","order":1116},
{"id":"p1-017","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What property does 'Redwood seconds' measure?","back":"Viscosity. It is the time in seconds for a fixed volume of oil to flow through a standard orifice of the Redwood viscometer.","explain":"Viscosity matters for fuel oils because it sets the pumping and atomisation temperature. Furnace oil is preheated to bring its viscosity down to the level the burner needs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q3","order":1117},
{"id":"p1-018","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Which common substance has the highest specific heat, and what is its value?","back":"Water, at about 1 kcal/kg°C (4.187 kJ/kg·K). It is far higher than metals such as iron (~0.11), mercury (~0.033) or lead (~0.03).","explain":"Water's high specific heat makes it an excellent heat-transfer and storage medium, used in cooling towers, hot-water systems and chilled water. Heating 500 litres of water (500 kg) by 6°C needs 500 × 1 × 6 = 3,000 kcal, which is about 12,560 kJ.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q7, Q10; BEE 2024 P1 Q8","order":1118},
{"id":"p1-019","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What is the formula for sensible heat, and how does latent heat differ?","back":"Sensible heat Q = m × Cp × ΔT, which changes temperature without a change of phase. Latent heat Q = m × L, which changes phase (e.g. boiling or condensing) at constant temperature.","explain":"Example: cooling 2,000 kg of water by 10°C removes 2,000 × 1 × 10 = 20,000 kcal of sensible heat. Condensing steam releases its latent heat (about 540 kcal/kg at atmospheric pressure), which is why steam is such an effective heating medium.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q10","order":1119},
{"id":"p1-020","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What is the dryness fraction (x) of dry saturated steam, and what does x < 1 mean?","back":"x = 1 for dry saturated steam (BEE also gives x = 1 for superheated steam). x < 1 means wet steam that carries water droplets: x = mass of dry steam ÷ total mass.","explain":"Wet steam has less usable enthalpy per kg (hf + x·hfg). It also causes erosion and poorer heat transfer, so steam traps and separators are used to keep steam dry.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q39","order":1120},
{"id":"p1-021","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How do you calculate the number of moles in a given mass? (e.g. 72 g or 54 g of water)","back":"Moles = mass ÷ molecular weight. Water (H₂O) has a molecular weight of 18, so 72 g = 4 mol and 54 g = 3 mol.","explain":"Mole calculations underpin combustion stoichiometry. For example, C + O₂ → CO₂ means 12 kg of carbon gives 44 kg of CO₂, so emissions = carbon × 44/12.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q9; BEE 2025 P1 Q46","order":1121},
{"id":"p1-022","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What is the mean molecular weight of air (77% N₂, 23% O₂ by weight)?","back":"About 28.8 (≈ 29) g/mol. Calculation: 1 ÷ (0.77/28 + 0.23/32) ≈ 28.8.","explain":"When composition is given by weight, use the harmonic mean: M = 1 ÷ Σ(wᵢ/Mᵢ). By volume, air is about 79% N₂ and 21% O₂, and the mole-weighted average gives the same ≈ 28.8–29.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q50","order":1122},
{"id":"p1-023","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Which fuel has the largest difference between GCV and NCV, and why?","back":"Natural gas. Its high hydrogen content forms a lot of water vapour on combustion, and the latent heat of that vapour is counted in GCV but not in NCV.","explain":"GCV (HHV) assumes the water vapour in flue gas is condensed and its latent heat recovered. NCV (LHV) assumes it leaves as vapour. Fuels rich in hydrogen (natural gas, LPG) or moisture show the biggest gap. BEE efficiency calculations conventionally use GCV.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q27","order":1123}
]$cards$::jsonb);
